-- Articles
-- Group articles are created by maintainers.
-- Item articles are created by students and use the Project type.

INSERT INTO article (
    title,
    content,
    user_id,
    item_id,
    group_id,
    type_id
) VALUES

-- ============================================================
-- GROUP ARTICLES
-- ============================================================

(
    'Getting Started with Raspberry Pi 4',
    '# Getting Started

The Raspberry Pi 4 is a small single-board computer suitable for a wide range of projects.

## Basic setup

1. Connect the power supply.
2. Connect a display and keyboard if needed.
3. Insert the prepared SD card.
4. Power on the board.

Make sure the board has adequate cooling during longer workloads.',
    1, NULL, 2, 1
),
(
    'Arduino Uno Basics',
    '# Arduino Uno Basics

The Arduino Uno is a simple microcontroller board commonly used for learning electronics.

## First project

A good first project is a blinking LED.

Connect an LED through a resistor and configure one of the digital pins as an output.

Always disconnect power before changing the wiring.',
    1, NULL, 3, 1
),
(
    'ESP32 Wi-Fi Projects',
    '# ESP32 Wi-Fi Projects

The ESP32 combines a microcontroller with Wi-Fi and Bluetooth.

## Common uses

- IoT projects
- Wireless sensors
- Web servers
- Bluetooth controllers
- Remote monitoring

For reliable wireless operation, use a good USB cable and a stable power source.',
    1, NULL, 4, 3
),
(
    'Measuring Temperature',
    '# Measuring Temperature

Temperature sensors are useful for monitoring environmental conditions and electronics projects.

## Tips

Place the sensor away from components that generate significant heat.

Allow the sensor to stabilise before recording measurements.

> Measurements may vary slightly depending on the environment and sensor placement.',
    1, NULL, 5, 2
),
(
    'Ultrasonic Distance Measurement',
    '# Ultrasonic Distance Measurement

Ultrasonic sensors measure distance by sending out a short sound pulse and measuring the return time.

## Example uses

- Obstacle detection
- Robot navigation
- Automatic doors
- Tank level measurement

Measurements work best when the target surface is relatively flat and perpendicular to the sensor.',
    1, NULL, 6, 4
),
(
    'Using the Laboratory Webcams',
    '# Using the Laboratory Webcams

The laboratory webcams can be connected through USB and used by most applications that support standard webcams.

## Troubleshooting

If the image is too dark, check the exposure settings.

If autofocus behaves unexpectedly, try switching to manual focus if the application supports it.',
    1, NULL, 7, 4
),
(
    '3D Printing Workflow',
    '# 3D Printing Workflow

A typical 3D printing workflow is:

1. Prepare the model.
2. Slice the model.
3. Check the printer.
4. Level the build plate.
5. Start the print.
6. Monitor the first layers.

## Before printing

Check that the build plate is clean and that the nozzle is free of old material.',
    1, NULL, 8, 1
),
(
    'Soldering Station Guide',
    '# Soldering Station Guide

The soldering stations have adjustable temperature controls.

## Basic procedure

1. Select an appropriate temperature.
2. Allow the station to heat up.
3. Tin the tip if necessary.
4. Solder the connection.
5. Clean the tip.
6. Return the station to a safe temperature.

Never leave a heated soldering iron unattended.',
    1, NULL, 9, 1
),
(
    'Using a Multimeter',
    '# Using a Multimeter

A multimeter can measure quantities such as voltage, resistance, and current.

## Before measuring

Check:

- The selected measurement mode
- Probe connections
- Expected measurement range
- Maximum rated input

> Never connect the meter to a circuit using the wrong measurement mode.',
    1, NULL, 10, 4
),
(
    'Stepper Motor Basics',
    '# Stepper Motor Basics

Stepper motors move in discrete steps and are commonly used in robotics and automation projects.

## Using a stepper motor

A suitable motor driver is required. The motor should not be connected directly to a microcontroller.

Motors can become warm during extended operation, so allow sufficient ventilation.',
    1, NULL, 11, 1
),

-- ============================================================
-- ITEM ARTICLES
-- All created by students (users 3-10).
-- All are Project articles (type 3).
-- ============================================================

(
    'Raspberry Pi 4: Simple Web Server',
    '# Simple Web Server

This project uses the Raspberry Pi to host a small local web server.

## Goal

Install a lightweight web server and make a test page accessible from another computer on the same network.

## Notes

Keep the Pi connected to a stable network connection during testing.',
    3, 1, NULL, 3
),
(
    'Raspberry Pi 4: Temperature Monitor',
    '# Temperature Monitor

Build a simple temperature monitor using the Raspberry Pi and a temperature sensor.

## Project idea

Read the sensor periodically and display the current temperature on a small web page.

This combines GPIO, sensors, and basic networking.',
    4, 2, NULL, 3
),
(
    'Arduino Uno: LED Traffic Light',
    '# LED Traffic Light

Create a small traffic light using three LEDs.

## Components

- Arduino Uno
- Red LED
- Yellow LED
- Green LED
- Resistors

Program the LEDs so that they change in a realistic traffic-light sequence.',
    5, 3, NULL, 3
),
(
    'Arduino Uno: Digital Pin Test',
    '# Digital Pin Test

This project tests the digital pins of an Arduino Uno.

## Procedure

Connect an LED through a resistor and test the pins one at a time.

Record which pins behave as expected and which require further investigation.',
    6, 4, NULL, 3
),
(
    'ESP32: Wi-Fi Temperature Display',
    '# Wi-Fi Temperature Display

Use the ESP32 to read a temperature sensor and publish the result over Wi-Fi.

## Overview

The ESP32 reads the sensor periodically and serves the latest value through a simple HTTP page.

This combines sensors, microcontrollers, and networking.',
    7, 5, NULL, 3
),
(
    'ESP32: Wireless Sensor Node',
    '# Wireless Sensor Node

Turn the ESP32 into a small wireless sensor node.

## Project

Connect a sensor and periodically transmit its readings to another computer on the network.

Use a stable USB connection during testing to avoid unexpected resets.',
    8, 6, NULL, 3
),
(
    'Temperature Sensor: Room Monitor',
    '# Room Temperature Monitor

Build a simple room temperature monitor.

## Project idea

Take a temperature reading every few seconds and display the current value.

For better results, keep the sensor away from computers, power supplies, and other heat-producing equipment.',
    9, 7, NULL, 3
),
(
    'Temperature Sensor: Comparing Measurements',
    '# Comparing Temperature Measurements

Use this sensor to compare temperatures at different locations.

## Experiment

Measure the temperature:

1. Near a window.
2. In the centre of the room.
3. Near a computer.
4. Near a power supply.

Record the results and discuss why the measurements differ.',
    10, 8, NULL, 3
),
(
    'Ultrasonic Sensor: Parking Assistant',
    '# Parking Assistant

Create a simple parking assistant using an ultrasonic sensor.

## Behaviour

The closer an object gets to the sensor, the faster an LED should blink.

This provides a simple introduction to distance measurement and conditional logic.',
    3, 9, NULL, 3
),
(
    'Ultrasonic Sensor: Obstacle Detector',
    '# Obstacle Detector

Build a small obstacle detector using the ultrasonic sensor.

## Example

Use the sensor to detect objects in front of a robot and stop the robot when the measured distance falls below a selected threshold.',
    4, 10, NULL, 3
),
(
    'Webcam: Motion Monitoring',
    '# Motion Monitoring

Use the webcam to monitor a small area.

## Project idea

Capture frames periodically and compare them to detect changes.

This can be extended into a simple motion-detection system.',
    5, 11, NULL, 3
),
(
    'Webcam: Document Camera',
    '# Document Camera

Turn the webcam into a simple document camera.

## Setup

Position the camera above the workspace and adjust the exposure and focus.

This can be useful for recording electronics work or demonstrating a circuit to others.',
    6, 12, NULL, 3
),
(
    '3D Printer: Calibration Cube',
    '# Calibration Cube

Print a small calibration cube to evaluate the printer.

## What to check

- Overall dimensions
- Layer consistency
- First-layer adhesion
- Corner quality
- Stringing

Record the results before making adjustments to the printer settings.',
    7, 13, NULL, 3
),
(
    '3D Printer: First Layer Test',
    '# First Layer Test

This project focuses on getting a reliable first layer.

## Procedure

1. Clean the build plate.
2. Check the bed levelling.
3. Start a small test print.
4. Observe the first layer.
5. Adjust the printer if necessary.

A good first layer is one of the most important factors in a successful print.',
    8, 14, NULL, 3
),
(
    'Soldering Station: LED Circuit',
    '# LED Circuit

Build a simple LED circuit using the soldering station.

## Project

Solder an LED, resistor, and connector onto a small prototyping board.

Allow the soldering station to reach the selected temperature before beginning.',
    9, 15, NULL, 3
),
(
    'Soldering Station: Cable Repair',
    '# Cable Repair

Practice repairing a damaged low-voltage cable.

## Procedure

Strip the damaged section, prepare the conductors, solder the connection, and insulate it properly.

Always work on cables that are disconnected from their power source.',
    10, 16, NULL, 3
),
(
    'Multimeter: Resistor Measurements',
    '# Resistor Measurements

Use the multimeter to measure several resistors and compare the results with their labelled values.

## Experiment

Measure at least five resistors and calculate the difference between the measured and expected values.

Remember to select resistance mode before measuring.',
    3, 17, NULL, 3
),
(
    'Multimeter: Checking a Power Supply',
    '# Checking a Power Supply

Use the multimeter to check the output voltage of a low-voltage power supply.

## Procedure

1. Check the expected voltage.
2. Select an appropriate voltage range.
3. Connect the probes correctly.
4. Measure the output.

Never exceed the rated input limits of the multimeter.',
    4, 18, NULL, 3
),
(
    'Stepper Motor: Basic Rotation',
    '# Basic Stepper Motor Rotation

Connect the stepper motor to a suitable driver and make it rotate a fixed number of steps.

## Goal

Learn how step count, direction, and speed affect the motor.

Do not connect the motor directly to a microcontroller.',
    5, 19, NULL, 3
),
(
    'Stepper Motor: Speed Experiment',
    '# Stepper Motor Speed Experiment

Investigate how motor speed affects vibration and movement.

## Experiment

Run the motor at several different speeds and record:

- Speed
- Direction
- Vibration
- Temperature
- Missed steps

Compare the results and determine a suitable operating range.',
    6, 20, NULL, 3
);

